## 3. Function Descriptions

### Constants

- **`MAX_STORED_POLYNOMIAL_DEGREE_V1` (35)** [C1]: Maximum degree of a
  polynomial that will be stored/serialized for Protocol V1. A degree-35
  polynomial has 36 coefficients.
- **`MAX_INTERMEDIATE_POLYNOMIAL_DEGREE_V1` (36)** [C2]: Maximum degree of a
  polynomial that may appear during intermediate Lagrange interpolation
  calculations. One degree higher than the stored maximum.
- **`COMPLETE_POINTS_POLYS_1`** [C3]: Precomputed array of 1 Lagrange basis
  polynomial for 1 complete point.
- **`COMPLETE_POINTS_POLYS_3`** [C4]: Precomputed array of 3 Lagrange basis
  polynomials for 3 complete points.
- **`COMPLETE_POINTS_POLYS_5`** [C5]: Precomputed array of 5 Lagrange basis
  polynomials for 5 complete points.
- **`COMPLETE_POINTS_POLYS_30`** [C6]: Precomputed array of 30 Lagrange basis
  polynomials for 30 complete points.
- **`COMPLETE_POINTS_POLYS_34`** [C7]: Precomputed array of 34 Lagrange basis
  polynomials for 34 complete points.
- **`COMPLETE_POINTS_POLYS_36`** [C8]: Precomputed array of 36 Lagrange basis
  polynomials for 36 complete points.
- **`CHUNK_SIZE` (32)** [C9]: Size of an encoding chunk in bytes.
- **`NUM_POLYS` (16)** [C10]: Number of polynomials tracked per chunk;
  equals `CHUNK_SIZE / 2` (each GF16 element is 2 bytes).
- **`PolyConst::ZEROS`** [C11]: A `PolyConst<N>` with all coefficients set
  to `GF16::ZERO`. Used as the initial value when constructing arrays of
  `PolyConst` values.

### Pt (Cartesian Point)

- **`Pt::serialize`** [F1]: Serializes a GF(2¹⁶) cartesian point
  `Pt { x, y }` into a 4-byte big-endian array: `[x_hi, x_lo, y_hi, y_lo]`.
- **`Pt::deserialize`** [F2]: Deserializes a 4-byte big-endian array into a
  `Pt { x, y }` by reading two `u16` values via `from_be_bytes`.
- **`{impl Ord}::cmp`** [F3]: Total ordering on `Pt` by delegating to
  `partial_cmp` and unwrapping.
- **`{impl PartialOrd}::partial_cmp`** [F4]: Partial ordering on `Pt` by
  comparing `self.x.value` vs `other.x.value` as `u16`.
- **`{impl PartialEq}::eq`** [F5]: Equality on `Pt` by comparing
  `self.x.value == other.x.value` (y-coordinate ignored).

### Poly (Dynamic Polynomial)

- **`Poly::zero(capacity)`** [F6]: Creates an empty polynomial with the given
  Vec capacity. Used to pre-allocate result polynomials.
- **`Poly::lagrange_interpolate(pts)`** [F7]: Given a set of points with unique
  X values, returns a polynomial `p` such that `p(pts[i].x) == pts[i].y` for
  all `i`. Uses O(N²) work and O(N) space with the prepare/complete strategy.
- **`Poly::lagrange_interpolate_prepare(pts)`** [F8]: Computes
  `PRODUCT(x - pi.x)` for all points, building the polynomial from its
  largest coefficient down. Returns a template polynomial used by
  `lagrange_interpolate_complete`.
- **`Poly::mult_xdiff_assign_trailing(start, difference)`** [F9]: Multiplies
  the trailing coefficients `self[start..]` by `(x - difference)` in-place,
  propagating the carry into `self[start-1]`.
- **`Poly::lagrange_interpolate_complete(pts, i)`** [F10]: Given the product
  polynomial from `prepare`, divides out `(x - pts[i].x)` and scales the
  result so that `f(pts[i].x) == pts[i].y`. The result is scaled by `x`
  (callers handle this).
- **`Poly::lagrange_interpolate_pt(pts, i)`** [F11]: Convenience method that
  calls `prepare` + `complete` + removes the leading zero coefficient.
  Used as a fallback for encoding.
- **`Poly::add_assign(other)`** [F12]: Adds `other` to `self` coefficient-by-
  coefficient. Extends `self` if `other` has more coefficients.
- **`Poly::mult_assign(m)`** [F13]: Multiplies every coefficient by scalar `m`
  using `gf::parallel_mult`.
- **`Poly::compute_at(x)`** [F14]: Evaluates the polynomial at point `x` by
  computing `x^0, x^1, ..., x^N` via repeated squaring, then summing
  `coefficient[i] * x^i`. Marked `#[hax_lib::opaque]` due to `zip`.
- **`Poly::lagrange_sum(pts, polys)`** [F15]: Computes `SUM(polys[i] *
  pts[i].y)` to combine Lagrange basis polynomials into a single
  interpolating polynomial. Marked `#[hax_lib::opaque]` due to `zip`.
- **`Poly::from_complete_points(pts)`** [F16]: Given points with X values
  filling `[0..pts.len())`, returns the interpolating polynomial using
  precomputed Lagrange basis polynomials (for sizes 0, 1, 3, 5, 30, 34, 36)
  or falls back to `lagrange_interpolate_pt`. Marked `#[hax_lib::opaque]`.
- **`Poly::serialize`** [F17]: Serializes the polynomial as a byte vector:
  each coefficient's `u16` value is written in big-endian (2 bytes each).
- **`Poly::deserialize(serialized)`** [F18]: Deserializes a byte slice into
  a `Poly` by reading pairs of bytes as big-endian `u16` → `GF16`.
  Returns an error if the input is empty or has odd length.

### PolyConst (Fixed-Size Const Polynomial)

- **`PolyConst::lagrange_interpolate_pt(pts, i)`** [F19]: Const-evaluable
  version of Lagrange interpolation for a single point. Constructs the
  basis polynomial and scales it, using `const_sub`, `const_mul`, and
  `const_div` for GF16 operations.
- **`PolyConst::mult(m)`** [F20]: Const-evaluable scalar multiplication:
  multiplies every coefficient by `m` using `const_mul`.
- **`PolyConst::mult_xdiff(difference)`** [F21]: Const-evaluable multiplication
  by `(x - difference)`. Distributes as `x*poly - d*poly` using separate
  `xp` and `dp` arrays. Marked `#[hax_lib::opaque]`.
- **`PolyConst::to_poly`** [F22]: Converts a fixed-size `PolyConst<N>` to a
  dynamic `Poly` by calling `.to_vec()` on the coefficient array.

### Free Functions

- **`const_polys_to_polys(cps)`** [F23]: Converts an array of `PolyConst<N>`
  to a `Vec<Poly>` by mapping `to_poly` over the array via iterators.
- **`lagrange_polys_for_complete_points<N>()`** [F24]: Const-evaluable function
  that precomputes N Lagrange basis polynomials for points
  `{(0, 1), (1, 1), ..., (N-1, 1)}`. Used to initialize the
  `COMPLETE_POINTS_POLYS_*` constants.

### PolyEncoder

- **`PolyEncoder::get_encoder_state`** [F25]: Returns a reference to the
  internal `EncoderState` (either `Points` or `Polys`). Used in hax
  annotations.
- **`PolyEncoder::into_pb`** [F26]: Serializes the encoder state into a
  protobuf `PolynomialEncoder` message. Handles both `Points` and `Polys`
  variants.
- **`PolyEncoder::from_pb`** [F27]: Deserializes a protobuf `PolynomialEncoder`
  message back into a `PolyEncoder`. Validates field lengths and
  reconstructs either `Points` or `Polys` state.
- **`PolyEncoder::point_at(poly, idx)`** [F28]: Returns the GF16 value at
  position `(poly, idx)`. If the encoder is in `Points` state and the
  requested index exceeds stored points, converts to `Polys` state by
  calling `from_complete_points`, then evaluates via `compute_at`.
  Marked `#[hax_lib::opaque]`.
- **`PolyEncoder::encode_bytes_base(msg)`** [F29]: Encodes a byte message
  into the encoder's initial `Points` state. Splits the message into
  2-byte GF16 elements distributed round-robin across 16 polynomials.
- **`PolyEncoder::chunk_at(idx)`** [F30]: Generates the chunk at the given
  index by evaluating each of 16 polynomials at the appropriate point
  indices.
- **`PolyEncoder::into_pb_test`** [F31]: Test-utility wrapper for `into_pb`.
- **`PolyEncoder::from_pb_test`** [F32]: Test-utility wrapper for `from_pb`.
- **`{impl Encoder}::encode_bytes(msg)`** [F33]: Trait method that delegates
  directly to `encode_bytes_base`.
- **`{impl Encoder}::next_chunk`** [F34]: Generates the next chunk by calling
  `chunk_at(self.idx)` and incrementing the index.

### PolyDecoder

- **`PolyDecoder::get_pts_needed`** [F35]: Returns `self.pts_needed`.
  Used in hax annotations.
- **`PolyDecoder::necessary_points(poly)`** [F36]: Computes the number of
  points needed for polynomial `poly`: `pts_needed / 16` plus 1 if
  `poly < pts_needed % 16`.
- **`PolyDecoder::new_with_poly_count(len_bytes, _polys)`** [F37]: Creates
  a new decoder with `pts_needed = len_bytes / 2` and 16 empty
  `SortedSet<Pt>` arrays.
- **`PolyDecoder::into_pb`** [F38]: Serializes the decoder state into a
  protobuf `PolynomialDecoder` message.
- **`PolyDecoder::from_pb`** [F39]: Deserializes a protobuf
  `PolynomialDecoder` message back into a `PolyDecoder`. Validates
  lengths and reconstructs `SortedSet` arrays. Marked `#[hax_lib::opaque]`.
- **`PolyDecoder::into_pb_test`** [F40]: Test-utility wrapper for `into_pb`.
- **`PolyDecoder::from_pb_test`** [F41]: Test-utility wrapper for `from_pb`.
- **`{impl Decoder}::new(len_bytes)`** [F42]: Trait method that delegates
  to `new_with_poly_count(len_bytes, 16)`.
- **`{impl Decoder}::add_chunk(chunk)`** [F43]: Adds a chunk's 16 GF16
  values to the appropriate `SortedSet<Pt>` arrays, keyed by polynomial
  index and point index. Only adds if the point is needed or has a
  small index (to enable direct lookup without interpolation).
- **`{impl Decoder}::decoded_message`** [F44]: Attempts to decode the
  original message from collected points. Uses direct lookup for points
  with matching X values, falling back to Lagrange interpolation. Returns
  `None` if insufficient points are available.

